{"frequently_observed_input_symbols": "Frequently observed input symbols are those symbols or data points that appear often in the input data, potentially leading to disruptions or oscillations in the learning process due to their high frequency."},
{"experiment_3__serves_to_illustrate__that_lstm_does_not_encounter_fundamental_problems_if_noise_and_signal_are_mixed_on_the_same_input_line": "The phrase \"serves to illustrate\" indicates that Experiment 3 was conducted with the purpose of demonstrating or providing evidence for a particular point or concept related to LSTM performance."},
{"that_lstm_does_not_encounter_fundamental_problems_if_noise_and_signal_are_mixed_on_the_same_input_line": "The statement \"that LSTM does not encounter fundamental problems if noise and signal are mixed on the same input line\" is the key finding or conclusion that Experiment 3 aims to demonstrate. It suggests that LSTM is capable of effectively processing inputs where noise and meaningful signal are combined."},
{"experiment_3__initially_focuses_on__bengio_et_al_s_simple_1994_two_sequence_problem": "Initially focuses on indicates that at the beginning of Experiment 3, the primary attention is directed towards a specific problem or dataset to assess the LSTM's performance and behavior under those conditions."},
{"bengio_et_al_s_simple_1994_two_sequence_problem": "Bengio et al.\u2019s simple 1994 two-sequence problem is a benchmark problem proposed by Bengio and colleagues in 1994. It involves processing two sequences to test the ability of neural networks to handle temporal dependencies and is used here as a starting point for Experiment 3 to evaluate LSTM\u2019s performance."},
{"experiment_3c__poses__a_more_challenging_two_sequence_problem": "The predicate \"poses\" in this context indicates that Experiment 3c presents or introduces a particular challenge or problem to be solved. It signifies that the experiment is designed to test the LSTM network's ability to handle a specific type of problem."},
{"a_more_challenging_two_sequence_problem": "The object \"a more challenging two-sequence problem\" refers to a complex version of the two-sequence problem initially introduced by Bengio et al. in 1994. This problem is designed to be more difficult than the original, testing the LSTM network's capabilities further."},
{"task_3a": "Task 3a is a specific classification task designed to observe and categorize input sequences into two distinct classes based on their initial elements.|Here is another description: Task 3a is a specific problem within a series of tasks, designed to test the ability to process and categorize sequences based on given criteria. It is part of a structured set of challenges aimed at evaluating sequence classification skills.|Here is another description: Task 3a is a computational task that serves as a baseline or reference for Task 3b, featuring a specific architecture and parameters without the addition of gaussian noise.|Here is another description: Task 3a is a baseline machine learning task that serves as a comparison point for Task 3c. It involves similar architecture and parameters but without the additional complexities introduced in Task 3c, such as specific target values and Gaussian noise."},
{"task_3a__is__two_sequence_problem": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the identification or definition of Task 3a, linking it to its specific problem type, which is the Two-Sequence Problem."},
{"two_sequence_problem": "The Two-Sequence Problem is a classification challenge where input sequences are observed and categorized into one of two classes. The classification is based on the initial elements of the sequences, with subsequent elements following a Gaussian distribution. The problem defines correct classification as having an absolute output error below 0.2 at the sequence end."},
{"task_3a__involves__observing_and_classifying_input_sequences": "The predicate \"involves\" signifies that Task 3a requires or consists of certain actions or processes. In this context, it indicates that the task is composed of observing and classifying input sequences according to predefined rules and conditions."},
{"observing_and_classifying_input_sequences": "Observing and classifying input sequences refers to the process of examining sequences of data points and determining their category based on specific characteristics. In Task 3a, this involves analyzing the first _N_ elements of the sequence to classify it into one of two classes, with classification accuracy measured by the output error at the sequence end."},
{"input_sequences": "Input sequences refer to the series of real-valued elements that are observed and classified in the two-sequence problem. These sequences are the primary data points used to determine their classification."},
{"input_sequences__have__two_classes": "The predicate \"have\" in this context signifies the possession or inclusion of a characteristic by the input sequences. Specifically, it indicates that the sequences are categorized into distinct groups based on their properties."},
{"each_class": "Each class refers to the two distinct categories into which input sequences are classified in the described task. These classes are differentiated based on the values of the first _N_ sequence elements.|Here is another description: Each class refers to the distinct categories or groups into which sequences are classified based on the temporal order of specific symbols within them."},
{"each_class__occurs_with__probability_0_5": "The predicate \"occurs with\" indicates the likelihood or probability associated with each class appearing in the classification task. It signifies how often each class is expected to be observed under the given conditions."},
{"probability_0_5": "The object \"probability 0.5\" specifies that each class has an equal chance of occurring, meaning there is a 50% probability for either class to be the correct classification for any given input sequence."},
