{"col_3__represents__weight_initialization_interval": "The term \"represents\" in this context indicates that Col. 3 is used as a label or identifier for the data it contains. It signifies that the data found in Col. 3 is specifically related to the weight initialization interval for the tasks or experiments being documented."},
{"weight_initialization_interval": "The weight initialization interval refers to the range or set of values used to initialize the weights in a neural network or similar machine learning model before training begins. This interval is crucial as it can significantly influence the convergence and performance of the model during the training process."},
{"col_4__represents__test_set_size": "The term \"represents\" in this context signifies that Col. 4 is used as a placeholder or indicator for a particular variable or parameter within the dataset."},
{"test_set_size": "Test set size is a parameter that indicates the number of data points or instances that are reserved for testing the performance of a model after it has been trained. It is an important factor in evaluating the generalization capability of the model."},
{"col_5__represents__stopping_criterion_for_training": "The term \"represents\" in this context indicates that Col. 5 is used to signify or stand for a particular condition or criterion that is essential for the process being described, which in this case is the stopping criterion for training."},
{"stopping_criterion_for_training": "The stopping criterion for training is a condition or set of conditions that determine when the training process of a model should be halted. In this context, it is specifically related to achieving an average training error below a certain threshold (\u03b2) and ensuring that the 2000 most recent sequences were processed correctly."},
{"st_3_\u03b2": "_ST_ 3 _(\u03b2)_ is a specific stopping criterion used in the context of training machine learning models, particularly those involving sequence processing. It is designated to ensure that the model has reached a satisfactory level of performance before halting the training process."},
{"st_3_\u03b2__stands_for__average_training_error_below_\u03b2_and_the_2000_most_recent_sequences_were_processed_correctly": "\"stands for\" is a relational term used to indicate that _ST_ 3 _(\u03b2)_ is an abbreviation or shorthand representation for a more detailed concept or condition. In this case, it explains the precise meaning and requirements associated with the stopping criterion _ST_ 3 _(\u03b2)_."},
{"average_training_error_below_\u03b2_and_the_2000_most_recent_sequences_were_processed_correctly": "\"average training error below _\u03b2_ and the 2000 most recent sequences were processed correctly\" describes a dual condition for stopping the training of a machine learning model. The first part requires that the average error during training falls below a specified threshold _\u03b2_, indicating a desired level of accuracy. The second part mandates that the model has successfully processed the most recent 2000 sequences without error, ensuring recent performance stability."},
{"col_6__represents__success_correct_classification_criterion": "The predicate \"represents\" in this context signifies that Col. 6 is designated to embody or stand for the success (correct classification) criterion. It acts as a marker or indicator within the dataset to show whether the classification performed meets the predefined success standards."},
{"success_correct_classification_criterion": "The success (correct classification) criterion is a specific standard or threshold that must be met for a classification to be considered successful. In the context provided, it is detailed as \"absolute error of all output units at sequence end is below \u03b2,\" where \u03b2 is a predefined error threshold. This criterion ensures that the classification results are within an acceptable margin of error."},
{"abs_\u03b2": "_ABS(\u03b2)_ is a criterion used to determine the success of a classification task in a machine learning context. It specifically measures the performance of output units at the end of a sequence."},
{"abs_\u03b2__stands_for__absolute_error_of_all_output_units_at_sequence_end_is_below_\u03b2": "\"stands for\" indicates that _ABS(\u03b2)_ is an abbreviation or shorthand representation for a more detailed concept or condition."},
