{"section_4__describes__the_lstm_architecture": "The predicate 'describes' indicates that Section 4 provides a detailed explanation or overview of the LSTM architecture. It serves as the section where the core concept and design of LSTM are elaborated upon."},
{"section_5": "Section 5 of the article is dedicated to showcasing the practical applications and performance of the long short-term memory (LSTM) architecture. It serves as a critical part of the article where empirical evidence and comparisons are presented to validate the proposed LSTM method."},
{"section_5__presents__numerous_experiments_and_comparisons_with_competing_methods": "The predicate 'presents' in this context signifies the act of showcasing or displaying. It indicates that Section 5 of the article is the section where various experiments and comparisons are shown to the readers, serving as evidence of the effectiveness of the LSTM method."},
{"numerous_experiments_and_comparisons_with_competing_methods": "The object 'numerous experiments and comparisons with competing methods' refers to the detailed empirical evaluations and benchmarks conducted to test the performance of LSTM against other existing methods. These experiments and comparisons are crucial for demonstrating the superiority and unique capabilities of LSTM in handling complex tasks and bridging long time intervals."},
{"lstm__outperforms__competing_methods": "The term \"outperforms\" indicates that LSTM demonstrates superior performance compared to other methods in the context of sequence learning tasks. This superiority is measured in terms of effectiveness in learning and generalizing from data, especially in scenarios involving long-term dependencies."},
{"competing_methods": "Competing methods refer to other recurrent neural network algorithms and architectures that were previously used for sequence learning tasks. These methods include conventional backpropagation through time (BPTT) and real-time recurrent learning (RTRL), among others, which face challenges like vanishing or exploding gradients when dealing with long sequences."},
{"lstm__learns_to_solve__complex_artificial_tasks_no_other_recurrent_net_algorithm_has_solved": "The predicate \"learns to solve\" indicates LSTM's capability to effectively tackle and resolve complex problems that are difficult or impossible for other recurrent network algorithms to solve. This capability is a direct result of LSTM's unique architecture and learning algorithm, which allows it to handle long-term dependencies and noisy data more effectively."},
{"complex_artificial_tasks_no_other_recurrent_net_algorithm_has_solved": "The object \"complex, artificial tasks no other recurrent net algorithm has solved\" refers to a set of challenging problems specifically designed to test the capabilities of recurrent neural networks. These tasks are characterized by their complexity and the difficulty they pose to conventional algorithms, making them a benchmark for evaluating the effectiveness of new architectures like LSTM."},
{"section_6": "Section 6 is a part of the document that provides an analysis and discussion of the LSTM architecture. It follows sections that review previous work, analyze vanishing errors, introduce a naive approach to constant error backpropagation, describe the LSTM architecture, and present experiments and comparisons."},
{"section_6__discusses__lstm_s_limitations_and_advantages": "The predicate 'discusses' indicates that Section 6 provides a detailed examination and evaluation of certain aspects related to LSTM. It involves presenting both the positive and negative aspects, offering a balanced view on the topic."},
{"lstm_s_limitations_and_advantages": "LSTM\u2019s limitations and advantages refer to the constraints and benefits associated with the Long Short-Term Memory architecture. These could include issues like computational complexity, training difficulties, or specific scenarios where LSTM may not perform optimally, as well as its strengths such as handling long-term dependencies, improved gradient flow, and successful application in various sequence learning tasks."},
